What you are declaring Default if you leave the modifier off A class, struct, record, interface or enum at the top level <code>internal</code> A field, method or property inside a class <code>private</code> A member of an interface <code>public</code> (and before C# 8 it could not be anything else) A member of an enum <code>public</code> <p>So this:</p> <pre><code>class Foo
{
    int x;
}
</code></pre> <p>is exactly the same as this:</p> <pre><code>internal class Foo      // NOT public!
{
    private int x;
}
</code></pre> <p>That first one catches people out when building a class library. Your class compiles fine, but nobody outside your project can use it, and the error message ("inaccessible due to its protection level") does not obviously say "you forgot <code>public</code>".</p>
